WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.630
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.920
Thank you for joining
us at this town meeting.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.400
I'm Kim Goff-Crews,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.283
Secretary and Vice President
for University Life.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.820
Before I turn things over
to President Salovey,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.360
I wanna take a moment to talk to you

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.260
a little bit about the meeting format.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.867
We solicited questions in the (mumbles)

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.080
and we received hundreds.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.650
So we've grouped the questions

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.340
into a series of themes,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.970
and the presentation will address

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.880
many of those themes.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.330
We do know, however, that
the presentation itself

00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:41.667
may suggest that you have other questions,

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:43.930
and we ask that if you do,

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:46.610
that you fill out the form on the web page

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.730
that allowed you to
get into this broadcast

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.290
so that we can track them.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:51.370
If we have time,

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.800
we'll answer some of the questions live,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.940
but we will keep all the
questions and make sure

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.033
that they go to the
correct university leader.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.930
Now after today, the covid19.yale.edu

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.580
and Yale Today will continue to be

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.060
good sources of information,

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.883
so please check them often.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.550
And one final note,

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.440
this meeting is being recorded

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.380
because we wanna make sure that if people

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.270
are not available to see it,

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.870
that they have an opportunity to do so.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.890
And with that, I'm
going to turn everything

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.530
over to President Salovey.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:26.800
Thank you.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.553
- Well, thank you, Kim, and
good afternoon, everyone.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.770
I'm very grateful to be able to connect

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.970
with all of you during
this unsettling time

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.340
in New Haven, across the United States,

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:40.803
really around the globe.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.060
We've been seeing a global disruption

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.613
at a scale that hasn't been
experienced in generations.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.720
In recent months, we've
had to alter our lives

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.973
dramatically to contain
the spread of COVID-19.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.900
I know that many of you
have been confronting

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.730
the reality that intermediate

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.723
and long-term plans have been upended.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.230
You've made sacrifices,

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:10.113
you've experienced disappointments.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.850
Some of you have family members

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.250
and friends who are out of work

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.650
and who are worried about
supporting their families.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.000
And of course, some of you are caring

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.480
for someone who is sick,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.133
or you may be sick yourselves.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.980
I've lost someone in my extended family,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.400
and to those of you who
have also lost loved ones,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.763
I'm deeply, deeply sorry.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.530
Today though, I believe
it is also important

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.870
to express my gratitude
for the way our community

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.210
has come together to overcome challenges,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.190
to take care of one another,

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:47.503
to take care of our world.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:49.830
To the Yale community,

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.550
I'm thankful for your optimism,

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.453
for your resilience,
for your selflessness.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.610
Thank you for working hard to maintain

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.370
the core functions of our university,

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.183
especially during these challenging times.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.070
Our staff, whether on
campus or working remotely,

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.630
have really kept this place going.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.590
A faculty through their
willingness to teach

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.740
in new ways has ensured that we continue

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.790
to educate students and create knowledge.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.520
And our students have worked hard

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.373
to continue their learning online.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.030
To those of you who
are healthcare workers,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.223
you're our heroes.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.830
And I'm grateful to colleagues across Yale

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.090
who are coordinating with
the City of New Haven

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.433
and with the state of Connecticut as well.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.520
Working collaboratively with people

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.120
across our city and across our state,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.550
we can support one another.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.250
We can share research findings,

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.320
we can share best practices,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.473
we can share strategic planning.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.447
I believe we'll get through this,

00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:57.730
and we'll get through this especially

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.003
if we remain a unified Yale.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.590
In this period of uncertainty,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.330
I know that everyone wants as much clarity

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.920
as possible about what Yale will do

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.770
in the weeks and in the months ahead.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.230
For the next hour, my colleagues and I

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:17.190
will address your most pressing questions,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.123
and they fall into three themes:

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.010
the health and safety of our community,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.020
preparing for financial challenges,

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.823
and preparing for getting
the campus up and running.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.780
We'll then answer some of the questions

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.430
you submitted during the meeting.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:35.543
I have to say though,

00:04:35.543 --> 00:04:38.230
much of what we're gonna
talk about reflects

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.150
planning that is still in progress,

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.993
so you have to think of
this as a progress report.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.920
Okay, one of the most
commonly asked questions

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.223
is some version of how is the
university making decisions?

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.920
We wanna share with you
some of the key principles

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.523
that we use to plan and to take action.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.750
First, we will act to protect the health

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.150
and safety of Yale faculty, staff,

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.990
students, and neighbors.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.960
This is a first principle,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.870
all of our actions so far reflect the fact

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.353
that we view this as a
public health crisis first.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.510
Decisions about health and safety

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.760
made by the university in the context

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.780
of COVID-19 will be evidence-based,

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.983
data-driven to the extent possible.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.700
To me, this is what universities must do.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.620
We need to model what it
means to rely on experts,

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.530
on data, and on analysis
to decide among options.

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:48.213
This really isn't the
moment for gut feelings.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.320
Our decisions should sustain and advance,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.520
whenever possible, the quality of teaching

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:56.933
and research at Yale.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.183
We need to preserve our core mission,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.260
our missions that have engaged

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.053
this university for 319 years.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:10.110
We must take the long view in how we think

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:13.143
about our human and our
financial resources.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.270
And I wanna be clear,

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.223
we will be educating our
students in the fall.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.980
We don't know yet in what manner,

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.993
but we'll be teaching and
creating new knowledge.

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.970
The diversity and excellence
of our student body

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:32.350
is of fundamental importance,

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.370
and we won't compromise the ability

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.150
to educate students,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.013
regardless of family background.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.820
This means we need to
protect the financial

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.030
support that we provide to students,

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.573
especially those from the
least affluent families.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.210
Finally, the university-wide priorities

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:54.980
that I've been talking about for the past

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.850
few years have not changed,

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.090
and decision-making will continue

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:01.963
to reflect those priorities.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.070
Now what I'd like to do is move

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:08.400
to another frequently asked question.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.900
Many of you inquired about the status

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.507
of COVID-19 in New Haven,

00:07:13.507 --> 00:07:15.663
and what Yale is doing to combat it.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.650
So Nancy Brown is the
Jean and David Wallace

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:23.600
dean of the Yale School of Medicine,

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:24.843
and she's here with us.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:29.530
So, Nancy, how is Yale Medicine,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.430
the Yale medical school and our partners

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.360
at Yale New Haven Hospital prepared

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.890
to face down this virus?

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:39.750
Can you tell us a little
bit about their preparations

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.800
and the current situation,

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.700
the outlook for the virus in New Haven

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.870
and in the state of Connecticut,

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:46.880
and what's it gonna take,

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:48.500
what's it gonna take from a medical

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.800
and public health point of view

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.380
to resume activities on our campus?

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:55.213
Nancy.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.803
- Thank you, Peter.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.660
Let me start by framing where we are

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:04.190
and from whence we've come.

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:07.060
It's hard to believe but
the first case of COVID

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.603
was diagnosed in Connecticut on March 7th.

00:08:10.465 --> 00:08:12.160
And on the left, you can see the trend

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.450
in positive cases on the top,

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.150
and in the number of deaths per day

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:17.883
on the bottom in Connecticut.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:20.210
On the right, you see the number

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.410
of COVID-19 patients hospitalized

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.580
in our own Yale New Haven hospitals,

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.920
and as you can see, we are
seeing a downturn as well,

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:29.860
but that at our peak,

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.140
we had as many as 450 patients

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:35.100
in Yale New Haven Hospital
with approximately

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.670
70 of those patients on ventilators.

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:38.503
Next slide.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:41.770
There's been much work at Yale Health,

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:44.220
Yale Medicine, and Yale
New Haven Health System

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.610
leading up to the first case of COVID

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:48.920
and continuing to make
sure that we could care

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.080
for our patients safely.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:54.360
This included an early focus on testing,

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.520
and Marie Landry, shown here,

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.060
developed the first approved

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.320
viral tests in Connecticut,

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:00.920
and the first test at an academic

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:02.223
medical center anywhere.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.450
Yale Health prepared space for patients,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.770
and physicians such as Jonathan Siner

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:11.350
stood up three floors of
the Smilow Cancer center

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:13.060
as new intensive care space

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.330
for our COVID-positive patients.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.780
And we reduced the number
of elective surgeries

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:19.540
and outpatient visits to make room

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.330
for these patients.

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.840
We also innovated to make
sure that we have adequate

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.990
personal protective
equipment and ventilators.

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:27.823
Next.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.310
Early on, we realized that
we needed to coordinate

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.590
our research and our clinical responses

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.030
to fight COVID across
the School of Medicine,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:38.997
the hospital, and the university,

00:09:38.997 --> 00:09:42.620
and so we created the
COVID-19 Coordination

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:44.720
Response Team, or CoRreCT.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.740
And here I must give
credit to Ruth Montgomery

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:48.830
for the naming.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.550
And today this includes
members of the FAS,

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:53.670
the schools of engineering, medicine,

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:55.520
nursing, public health,

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.823
as well as the Yale New
Haven Health System.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.190
We created a web presence that could serve

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.700
as a repository for research protocols

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:06.670
and preprints so that our investigators

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.080
and clinicians could see data

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.120
as soon as it became available.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.240
And investigators at
Yale have been involved

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.440
in discovery science,

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.700
understanding the mechanism of infection,

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.030
and importantly, the host response,

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.910
as well as in vaccine development,

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.823
therapeutic trials, and
seminal epidemiologic studies.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:27.010
This webpage also provides continually

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:29.140
updated clinical protocols,

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.160
and these clinical
protocols have been informed

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.290
by conversations with colleagues in China,

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:35.447
Italy, Washington, and New York,

00:10:35.447 --> 00:10:37.970
and by our own evolving experience

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.520
as we learn about COVID-19.

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:41.353
Next slide.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:44.270
We know that the effects of COVID-19

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.230
are more severe in older patients

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.900
as well as those who have obesity

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.140
or diabetes and hypertension,

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.040
but we have also learned that the severity

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.040
of COVID-19 is greater in patients

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.730
of African or Hispanic descent.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.360
We've leveraged a wonderful group

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.270
called the Cultural Ambassadors.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.730
These are ministers from
the Connecticut AME Church

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:05.330
and members of Junta
for Progressive Action

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.190
who have been advocates for many years

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:09.870
for clinical research to get the word out

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.930
about the ways in which we
might limit COVID infections

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.860
and how patients can (murmurs).

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.090
We also understand that social distancing

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.270
and all the measures we
have taken to flatten

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.290
the curve have significant
effects on the economy,

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.940
and therefore the long-term
health of our community.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:26.970
And Yale economists and social scientists

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:28.650
have been advising local, state,

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.190
and national government on ways

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:32.100
to reduce the impact.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.510
A group of faculty from the
schools of public health,

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:36.100
nursing, medicine, and management,

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.870
as well as Yale Health
have been meeting daily

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:42.130
to address the conditions
necessary for reopening.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:43.890
One member of this group, Albert Ko,

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:47.123
is a member of the governor's
task force on reopening.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.930
Much has been written about the conditions

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.230
that are requisite, and
this chart on the left

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:55.080
is one that I find very simple but useful

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.010
from Resolve to Save Lives.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.420
And I think what you
can see from the graphs

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:00.780
that I showed at the beginning

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.280
that we are starting to
meet many of the conditions

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.520
such as the decline in the number of cases

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.033
and deaths in Connecticut.

00:12:08.033 --> 00:12:09.510
But we've also done a lot to ensure

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:13.400
that we have adequate
personal protective equipment.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.670
Many think tanks have issued reports

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:18.520
and we will use these as
well as our own expertise

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.190
on campus to guide our plans for reopening

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:25.500
in the context of both
national and state guidance.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:26.463
Peter, thank you.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.710
- I wrote to the Yale community

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:35.280
about the two big issues facing us

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.820
beyond the immediate health crisis,

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:41.030
how is COVID-19 affecting
Yale financially,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:43.483
and what are we planning
to do in the fall?

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:46.970
Provost Scott Strobel
is leading the effort

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.190
on both of these fronts,

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:50.650
and so why don't we turn to him.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:52.723
Scott, why don't we start with finances?

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:55.770
- Thanks, Peter.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:57.600
It's a pleasure to talk to you today

00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:58.913
about those questions.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.003
Like many of you,

00:13:02.144 --> 00:13:04.833
my household has been impacted by this.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:07.550
A few months ago, it was my wife and I

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:08.630
alone in our home.

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:10.910
We now have eight people living here

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:14.910
as my two adult children and their spouses

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:18.140
and their two children now live with us,

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.190
including a three-year-old granddaughter.

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:23.850
Turns out Nora's looking for distraction,

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:25.280
and one of the things she likes to do

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.300
is we every now and then go for a drive

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:30.870
to just get out of the
house for a few minutes.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:32.380
As we come to the driveway,

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.980
she begs from her car
seat that she might have

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:38.020
the opportunity to
steer into the driveway.

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:39.540
So before you call DCF,

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:41.103
this is just a few hundred,

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:45.360
a few dozen feet before
we reach the driveway,

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.360
and she needs to make a right-hand turn.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.400
What we've realized as she's done this

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.650
is she fails to recognize
that as we approach that turn,

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:55.840
she needs to begin turning the wheel.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:58.080
Instead, she assumes that she can begin

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.460
to turn as the corner is reached

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:02.943
rather than turning it in advance.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:08.280
In some sense, this is a allegory

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.530
of what it is that we're headed for

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:12.550
and what we're trying
to manage as we consider

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:14.790
the financial implications
that are now faced

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:15.690
by the university.

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:19.140
We do not yet know the full magnitude

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:21.740
of the turn that we need to make,

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:25.173
but we do know that we need
to start making that turn.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:28.580
Anybody who's looked at the headlines

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:32.100
in the past few weeks
recognizes that this pandemic

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:34.383
is causing huge economic impact.

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.440
Entire economies around
the world are in lockdown.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.890
Unemployment rates have now hit 30 million

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.810
in the United States alone,

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.500
which is an unemployment
rate approaching 20%,

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:48.140
which is the highest level since 1934

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:50.040
at the heart of the depression.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.660
Financial markets have fallen steeply,

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:54.870
and while they have recovered partially,

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.640
they are still highly
volatile and clearly unknown

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:01.230
for future days and weeks and months.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.160
The gross domestic products,

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:05.190
which is the broadest measure of goods

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.520
and services in the country,

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.000
fell by almost 5% in the first quarter,

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.690
which was not a full COVID quarter,

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:16.300
and it's likely to follow
still substantially

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:17.813
more in the coming quarter.

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.160
But what does all this mean for Yale?

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.230
What is the magnitude of the curve

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:24.180
that we need to make?

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.740
What is this turn we need to begin to make

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:28.853
and begin to follow?

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.260
Those are the questions that, of course,

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:33.760
we need to consider.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:34.803
And as I answer that question,

00:15:34.803 --> 00:15:38.350
I would like to consider
them in sort of three phases,

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:40.740
the first phase being
effectively this year,

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:45.070
this financial year,
which is fiscal year 20,

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.430
which began last July and continues

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:49.400
through June of this year.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.490
Then there is the medium term,

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:54.260
which is effectively
what happens next year.

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:55.480
And then the longer term,

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.600
which is what happens over the next

00:15:57.600 --> 00:15:58.853
one to five years.

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:03.430
As you know, Yale has four
main sources of funding.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:06.290
These include tuition, fundraising,

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.460
the generosity of our donors,

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.410
returns on the endowment,

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.420
and clinical medical services.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:16.860
Those are the main ways
that we receive revenue,

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.410
and use that revenue to
pay the various expenses

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:20.633
that run the university.

00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.383
Over the past few months,

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.670
just the months of March and April,

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:31.070
the unexpected, unanticipated
impact of COVID-19

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:34.413
on our budget is
approximately $200 million.

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:38.160
This is in the form of both reduced income

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.583
and new COVID-specific costs.

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:44.070
This includes the refund that we made

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:48.090
to the room and board for
students in our housing.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:51.790
It includes the reduced clinical revenue

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.200
as a result of performing fewer elective

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.170
procedures in our hospital.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:01.250
It includes fewer gifts
from our generous donors.

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:03.880
It includes the reduction
in sponsored awards

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:10.600
from the research that's
underway in our laboratories.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:12.970
We've put up a field hospital.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:14.010
That had a cost.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:16.220
We lost revenue as a result of the summer

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:18.920
programs that we normally
have for students

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.390
that visit Yale during the summer months.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:25.230
As a result, we see a
substantial reduction

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:26.550
in our revenue that of course

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.543
hits directly to our bottom line.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.290
The good news is the
university has sufficient

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:36.570
reserves that we can
manage this one-time shock.

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.960
We budget conservatively,
and we act prudently,

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.350
and so if this was a one-time impact,

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:44.350
we would be able to easily,

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:46.990
not easily, but we would be able to manage

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:48.423
the beginning of this turn.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:52.230
But we anticipate that
this is just the beginning

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:53.260
of the turn.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:56.180
There are other high
levels of uncertainty.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:58.710
Let me give you just a few examples.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.670
As we begin consideration of the FY 21,

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.150
the fiscal year 21 budget,

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:06.410
which is next year's financial budget,

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:08.840
we can already see that
there will be greater need

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:11.350
for financial aid for our students.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:14.230
There is a significant level
of tuition uncertainty,

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:17.230
particularly for students
that are from international

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:21.030
locations that may not
be able to get to campus.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:22.960
Not because of a lack of interest

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:24.880
or a lack of desire on our part,

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:27.490
but challenges with their visa status

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:29.170
and being able to get
the visas that they need,

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:30.383
the paperwork they need

00:18:30.383 --> 00:18:33.020
to be able to come to the country.

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:35.010
It will have impact on the endowment

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:36.750
that is still yet to be understood

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:38.930
in relation to the markets.

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.120
And it's also possible
that this will continue

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:44.693
to impact the gifts that
we receive from our donors.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:48.730
Because the magnitude
of the turn is unknown

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:50.710
and will not be known for several months

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:52.460
or even a couple of years,

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.780
we have begun to make the turn

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.660
without fully knowing its magnitude.

00:18:57.660 --> 00:18:59.450
And to begin that turn,

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:01.730
we have announced several
actions for the coming year,

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:03.930
and these were
announcements that were made

00:19:03.930 --> 00:19:07.010
just over a week and a half ago.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:11.020
It included a 5% reduction in spending.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:12.900
This is a reduction in the general account

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:15.660
spending that is used to fund programs

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:17.910
across the university.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.950
And this 5% reduction
will largely be achieved

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:22.700
through other things that we announced

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.300
at that same time,

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:26.740
namely a pause in hiring through next year

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.150
with few exceptions that are permitted

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:33.000
or allowed with appeal of the provost

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:34.223
or the dean of medicine,

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:38.060
a salary freeze for the coming year,

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:40.110
reduced spending on building projects

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:43.420
in which we removed
$300 million of capital

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:46.260
spending from projects
over the next two years,

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.470
as well as a reduction in travel and other

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:51.633
discretionary spending
for the coming year.

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:55.580
So that identifies the actions

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.970
that we are going to
take for the coming year,

00:19:57.970 --> 00:19:59.850
but then the question is what happens

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.780
in that period and beyond?

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:05.640
What happens over that
period of one to five years?

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:07.570
And that is the area in which there is

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:10.240
still high uncertainty.

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:12.680
How steep is the economic downturn?

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:15.180
How fast or slow will the recovery be,

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.240
and will that recovery be a U-shape,

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:19.393
or a V-shape, or a W-shape?

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:23.150
The answers to those kinds of questions

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:25.840
will obviously directly impact our ability

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.300
to handle this financial storm.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:30.750
How long does the pandemic last,

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:32.070
and how quickly is a treatment

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:33.460
or a vaccine identified?

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:34.950
Because first and foremost,

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:37.370
this is a public safety issue,

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:40.390
a public health issue with consideration

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:42.660
as a priority given to our faculty,

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:44.450
staff, and students.

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:47.280
And so we are trying to manage this first

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:51.920
and foremost with the community's health

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:53.523
and wellbeing in mind.

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:57.300
How large is the impact on the endowment,

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.170
which fundamentally is at the core

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:01.520
of what we can afford,
given the large fraction

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:03.510
of our budget that it provides.

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.630
And how does that impact get incorporated

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:07.610
into the budget over time?

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:09.080
That is a question that we are still

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:11.090
seeking to understand.

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:13.790
The question that is
most often asked of me

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:18.520
and probably most often
asked by many of us is,

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:20.290
why is it that we can't just spend

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:22.490
more of the endowment
to cover the financial

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:25.610
short we have experienced
or may experience

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:27.180
in the coming years?

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:30.660
Why can't we change the spending rule

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:34.740
so that we can cover
anything that might be needed

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:38.023
utilizing the large size
of the endowment to do so?

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.880
I think that's an interesting question,

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.140
and it's an important question.

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:46.060
It's a question that requires an answer

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:49.180
that appreciates the long-term priority

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:50.540
that the endowment provides,

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:54.490
the long-time support that the endowment

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:56.430
provides to the university.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:21:59.940
And so I turn it back to you, Peter,

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:02.250
somebody who was a provost for many years

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:05.480
and has been president
for still several more

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:07.540
to help us understand the importance

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.473
of the endowment in managing
our budgets and finances.

00:22:12.135 --> 00:22:13.550
- Great, thank you, Scott, and thank you

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:16.410
for that discussion of our budget.

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:17.440
So the question is,

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.330
why can't we spend more
from the endowment?

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:23.150
It is true, I'm asked that question a lot.

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:24.930
It's very important to recognize

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:28.230
that the endowment is
not a budget surplus.

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:30.063
It's not a rainy day fund.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:35.480
What the endowment is,
is a collection of gifts,

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:38.600
a collection of gifts
given to the university

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:40.723
by generous donors, mostly alumni,

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:44.223
over the course of the
university's history.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.750
And these gifts are
restricted by the donors

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:49.620
in how they can be used.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:52.450
For example, there are
gifts for financial aid,

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:54.460
there are gifts for professorships,

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:56.573
there are gifts for student activities.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:01.573
These gifts are invested
and they grow over time.

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:06.530
Based on a policy that ensures that future

00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:09.030
generations have the same financial

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:10.530
resources that we do now,

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:12.260
not more, not less,

00:23:12.260 --> 00:23:15.660
the same financial
resources that we do now,

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:18.463
we spend a certain amount
from the endowment every year.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.690
It's about 5 1/4% percent of the value

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:23.540
of the endowment.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.770
That's actually a lot of money.

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:30.123
This year, that's about $1.4 billion.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:34.160
It's more than a third
of our operating budget

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:36.390
of the entire university.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:39.010
And if we were to leave
out the medical school,

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:41.620
it's more than half of
the operating budget

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:43.170
for the rest of the university.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.460
So to do this year after year,

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.510
the endowment must grow,

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:51.900
enough to make up what we spend for it.

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:54.450
I'm sorry, enough to make
up what we spend from it,

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:57.030
plus inflation.

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:59.120
So the endowment has to grow,

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:02.963
on average, the amount
we spend plus inflation.

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:06.530
So I asked the investments office

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:09.240
to give me an estimate of what will happen

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:12.720
to the endowment this year, next year,

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:13.920
and the following years,

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.580
and they are expecting the following.

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:23.000
They are expecting a
recession for some time,

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:24.553
and eventually a recovery.

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:28.500
And when we look at the
amount of the endowment

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:31.780
we can spend based on their predictions,

00:24:31.780 --> 00:24:35.260
we can compare it to the amount we thought

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:38.793
we could spend before
the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:40.920
and there's a difference.

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:44.150
A few years from now,

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:48.150
we could have 300 to $400 million per year

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:52.093
less to spend than we had
originally anticipated.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:55.540
And that's why we're taking the actions

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:56.640
that we're taking now,

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:00.720
to blunt the impact of this
anticipated drop in funds,

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:02.780
as Scott described.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:05.620
This is the turn that we are anticipating

00:25:05.620 --> 00:25:07.393
well in advance of making it,

00:25:08.833 --> 00:25:11.230
and so it's the budgetary framework

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:12.643
within which we're working.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.070
We'll keep the community
updated as the financial

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:18.373
picture becomes clearer and clearer.

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:21.550
Let's turn though now to questions

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:24.400
about the future including
the fall semester.

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:26.430
Scott, I'd like to ask you frame

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:27.783
that issue as well.

00:25:29.467 --> 00:25:30.343
- Thanks.

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:34.300
So as the community knows,

00:25:34.300 --> 00:25:39.200
I started as provost
on January 1st in 2020.

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:42.563
This was not particularly
good timing on my part.

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:46.770
I had been in the job just over two months

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:48.630
when the pandemic hit.

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.220
I think I at that point had figured

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.400
out where my office was and had managed

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:53.993
to bring in some of my books.

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:57.440
But here we are.

00:25:57.440 --> 00:26:01.410
So in the course of the first
hundred days in the job,

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:02.970
and I should say that I've at least

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:04.720
been in the job longer than Nancy has.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:06.420
She started on February 1st,

00:26:06.420 --> 00:26:11.420
so we have done a lot
of on-the-job training.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:15.190
During the first hundred
days in this role,

00:26:15.190 --> 00:26:16.970
we have moved classes online.

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:19.180
That was an announcement on March 10th.

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:20.770
Of course, these are all announcements

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:22.820
that you and the community have received.

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:24.910
We asked the community to work

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:26.380
from home on March 16th.

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:28.350
We stopped non-essential research

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.160
in laboratories and libraries,

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:33.100
we've postponed commencement of reunions,

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:35.240
we canceled summer events and travel,

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:37.220
and extended the work from home policy

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:40.173
through the end of May
just over a week ago.

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:44.420
The second half of spring semester 2020

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:46.440
will be memorable to all of us.

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:48.900
It was the semester that we converted

00:26:48.900 --> 00:26:53.660
from a residential university
to doing online education.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:55.890
It was not perfect, but
I think it worked better

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.980
than many of us expected.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:01.600
We demonstrated how
resilient this community is,

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:06.600
and overall, I think it
was an impressive effort.

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:09.440
I would specifically
want to thank the faculty

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:11.810
and graduate students for their hard work

00:27:11.810 --> 00:27:14.530
in delivering their courses in a format

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:17.690
that none of us imagined
or even contemplated

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:19.073
as the semester began.

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:23.550
I mentioned I'm sure that I'm probably

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.960
not the only one who is managing demands

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:29.070
of a child in a house as you consider

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:31.930
how you have juggled the various pressures

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:35.480
on your time over these
past several weeks.

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:38.210
I want to thank you, I
want to thank the faculty,

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:41.110
the staff, and the
students for their patience

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:43.500
in a learning environment
that is very different

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:45.770
than what they imagined when we started.

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:48.413
Work has changed, teaching has changed.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:51.820
Critical research has continued.

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:54.880
That includes research
that's going on still

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.240
in our laboratories focused on COVID-19,

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.720
some of which Nancy described to you,

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:02.190
research to expand testing,

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:05.233
to engineer increased ventilator capacity,

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.740
to do clinical studies on drug treatments

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:10.690
that will lessen the
symptoms of this disease,

00:28:10.690 --> 00:28:12.210
and of course, the frontline doctors,

00:28:12.210 --> 00:28:14.240
nurses, and patient-facing staff

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:15.840
who are heroes in this pandemic.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.250
The question all of us want to know

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:20.750
is what happens next?

00:28:20.750 --> 00:28:21.900
The way I phrase it is,

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:24.943
what will I do on my
second 100 days in the job?

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.150
When will laboratories be reactivated

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:30.230
for non-COVID research?

00:28:30.230 --> 00:28:32.320
When will the library be open again

00:28:32.320 --> 00:28:35.073
so that we can do work in the social

00:28:35.073 --> 00:28:37.040
sciences and humanities?

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:39.970
When will we be able to go back to work?

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:43.030
Will the university be
open in the fall semester?

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:45.460
Will we be online or
will we welcome students

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.300
back to the campus?

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:50.780
These continue to be difficult questions,

00:28:50.780 --> 00:28:53.163
and we do not yet have final answers.

00:28:54.050 --> 00:28:56.580
But we are taking each
of them systematically,

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:59.730
carefully, expeditiously with input

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.820
from across the campus to understand

00:29:03.739 --> 00:29:06.039
what the answers to those
questions should be.

00:29:07.240 --> 00:29:09.500
Last week, President
Salovey announced a process

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.490
for addressing these important questions

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:13.490
that employs six faculty committees

00:29:13.490 --> 00:29:16.610
seeking input from over
65 different faculty

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:19.790
and additional senior administrators

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:22.700
who have experience from
across the university.

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:25.940
They will in turn or are
in turn still seeking

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:28.310
further consultation from faculty,

00:29:28.310 --> 00:29:31.630
students, and staff to
identify what the answers

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:33.810
to these questions should be.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:36.650
Let me briefly introduce
you to those committees,

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:37.810
the work that they're doing,

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:40.233
and the way they're
approaching their mandate.

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:43.600
First and foremost, we want to follow

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:45.200
the public health advice,

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:46.650
and so the first committee

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:49.330
is the Public Health Advisory Committee

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:51.920
that provides guidance for the protocols

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:55.600
and procedures and
directions we will follow

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.100
as we consider any other action

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:00.830
that the university will take.

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:03.620
The second is the
Research Continuity Group

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:05.540
recognizing that it will be possible

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:09.020
to reactivate our
laboratories and our libraries

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:11.600
before it will be possible
to reactivate the work

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.323
that goes on in our
classrooms and dining halls.

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:20.400
This group is defining how to do safe labs

00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:23.393
and libraries with clear
protocols and standards.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:28.920
There is also an Academic Continuity group

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:31.880
that is chaired by Pericles Lewis.

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:34.830
Pericles will be talking
to us about the details

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:36.053
of his work in a minute.

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:39.090
But I want to identify what that group

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:40.820
was charged to do.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:42.930
One of the challenges
that I have in thinking

00:30:42.930 --> 00:30:45.250
about the decision-making process in this

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:48.610
is that it all becomes a
question of probabilities

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.760
of certain things falling into line.

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:53.440
Will there be sufficient testing?

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:55.527
Will there be sufficient PPE?

00:30:57.228 --> 00:31:00.470
And so to pull apart
that probability question

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:03.290
and make it possible to just think clearly

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:06.100
about the possibilities in front of us,

00:31:06.100 --> 00:31:07.930
we have asked one of the subcommittees

00:31:07.930 --> 00:31:10.830
under Academic Continuity
to think about the question,

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:13.920
what is the best version
of an online education

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:15.103
that we can provide,

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:18.410
recognizing that we
now have several months

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:20.720
to prepare for it if that's what we end up

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:22.200
needing to do in the fall?

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:24.373
So that's the thinking of one group.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:27.390
There's also a second
group that is thinking

00:31:27.390 --> 00:31:29.720
about the question,
what is the best version

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:32.510
of a safe residential in-person education

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:34.320
that we can provide in the fall,

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:37.100
recognizing that we will
be under constraints

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:40.450
for public health considerations?

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:43.330
Those two considerations, those two groups

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:46.030
thinking in parallel with each other

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:49.750
we think will (murmurs)
the best possible version

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:52.700
of what eventually becomes our decision

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:54.053
for the fall semester.

00:31:55.520 --> 00:31:57.030
There's also a group considering

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:58.840
what are we doing in
the creative practice,

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:00.950
specifically around the arts?

00:32:00.950 --> 00:32:04.090
For example, this
represents particularly new

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:05.210
and different questions,

00:32:05.210 --> 00:32:06.890
different than what's being considered

00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:08.550
in the other two groups:

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:10.470
How do you socially distance a play,

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:13.100
or how do you socially
distance an orchestra?

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:15.520
So what it means to bring the arts groups,

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:19.070
reactivate the arts groups
is an important question.

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:21.160
All of this will be informed by a group

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:22.820
that's thinking about operations.

00:32:22.820 --> 00:32:24.520
How do we reimagine work?

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:27.240
How do we reimagine teaching and research?

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:29.290
And this group has considerations

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:31.719
from human resources facilities,

00:32:31.719 --> 00:32:33.483
IT, and hospitality.

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:37.480
Of course, we need to make an answer soon,

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:39.210
and the sooner we can make an answer,

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:40.960
the better it will be for our community

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:43.620
as you plan for what will
happen in coming days,

00:32:43.620 --> 00:32:44.633
weeks, and months.

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.130
We are committed to giving an answer

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:52.660
for what will happen in the fall semester

00:32:52.660 --> 00:32:55.090
no later than early July.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:58.787
So this will be approximately
two months from now,

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:01.840
giving us approximately two months

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:05.290
to then make final plans
for what the fall semester

00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:06.123
will look like.

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:11.180
With those committees at work,

00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:14.480
I should say that they are
working incredibly hard.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:16.580
They have made major time commitments

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:17.853
to get this work done,

00:33:18.704 --> 00:33:21.190
and I am very impressed
with the deliberations

00:33:21.190 --> 00:33:22.990
that they are already making,

00:33:22.990 --> 00:33:25.050
but of course, we all
want to know the answer

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:28.070
to the questions that they
have been tasked to answer.

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:28.903
Peter.

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:34.450
- So I think we should turn to Pericles

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:37.890
and ask him to tell us a little bit

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:41.970
particularly about the
work of his committee

00:33:41.970 --> 00:33:45.200
and what it is planning with
respect to the educational

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:46.500
mission of the university.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:50.190
- Thank you, Peter and Scott.

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:53.180
Yale, of course, has been
educating young people

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:55.543
for service for 319 years.

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:59.360
The educational mission of
the university is paramount,

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:01.970
second only to the health
and safety of our staff,

00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:03.760
our faculty, and our students,

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:05.840
and we need to be prepared to continue

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:08.540
this mission in the fall semester.

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:10.670
Like many peer colleges and universities,

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:12.380
we're looking at two main scenarios

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:13.890
for planning purposes,

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:16.490
and we'll probably wind up
with a hybrid of the two.

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:20.020
If we are unable to come
together in person in the fall,

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:22.970
we will provide remote or online options.

00:34:22.970 --> 00:34:25.630
That's, in a sense, scenario one.

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:27.460
We realize that this is not ideal.

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:29.740
I've been a professor
at Yale for 22 years,

00:34:29.740 --> 00:34:32.540
and I know that nobody comes here

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:34.140
in order to take online courses,

00:34:34.140 --> 00:34:37.240
or you wanna be here in
order to meet the faculty

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:38.783
and interact with your peers.

00:34:39.970 --> 00:34:41.710
But we know that some students,

00:34:41.710 --> 00:34:43.740
including some international students,

00:34:43.740 --> 00:34:45.290
or those with health issues,

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:46.990
or those otherwise unable to travel

00:34:46.990 --> 00:34:48.900
may not be able to make it to New Haven

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:50.840
in time for the start of classes,

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:52.500
and there's the risk of a second wave

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:56.280
of infection that might
delay in-person opening.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:59.060
So we're preparing for
the online possibility.

00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:00.990
Our faculty and staff worked very hard

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:04.440
over spring break to
get the spring semester

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:08.440
courses up and running online
in an emergency situation.

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:10.460
There were challenges,
but as Scott mentioned,

00:35:10.460 --> 00:35:11.870
I think the outcome was better

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:14.360
than many of us had expected.

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:16.790
We now have the summer
ahead of us to make sure

00:35:16.790 --> 00:35:18.320
that we provide this form of education

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:20.870
even more efficiently and effectively,

00:35:20.870 --> 00:35:23.250
and this means improvements in technology

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:25.070
and course design.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:26.730
So I'm grateful to staff and faculty

00:35:26.730 --> 00:35:28.410
who are working together to prepare

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:31.140
more effective online and remote versions

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:34.313
of courses for the fall,
should they be necessary.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:37.940
The second option, of
course, is to meet in person,

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:39.890
and if the public health
conditions warrant it,

00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:41.940
we'll be providing that
in-person education,

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:45.710
including residential
and extracurricular life.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:47.220
At spring break we had to close down

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:49.300
the residential colleges
and some other facilities

00:35:49.300 --> 00:35:51.050
very, very quickly.

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:53.370
The reopening is likely
to be more gradual,

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:54.930
and we'll need time to be prepared

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:57.230
for evolving circumstances,

00:35:57.230 --> 00:35:59.540
and also for any public health conditions

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:01.180
such as social distancing and so on

00:36:01.180 --> 00:36:02.960
that may be necessary,

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:05.890
even if we are back
here in person together.

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:07.930
There's a lot of uncertainty,

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:11.410
but our goal has been
to make the campus ready

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:13.180
for students to return
sometime in the fall,

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:15.300
as early as possible in the fall.

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:17.370
Initially this may be in smaller groups

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:19.167
and we may have some hybrid of in-person

00:36:19.167 --> 00:36:20.290
and online teaching.

00:36:20.290 --> 00:36:22.700
It won't be exactly the
same in the graduate

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:25.970
and professional schools
as it is in the college.

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:28.070
We're looking at safe
ways of housing students,

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:30.270
including providing
facilities for quarantine

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:33.090
and for self-isolation,
enabling contact tracing

00:36:33.090 --> 00:36:34.810
in case there's a second
wave of infection,

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:36.900
and of course, being prepared to treat

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:38.293
anyone who falls ill.

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:41.410
We plan to keep the campus informed

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:43.570
as this discussion proceeds,

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.820
and we'll provide more
detail in early summer.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:47.330
I realize that it's frustrating

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:49.480
not to be able to give a detailed account

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:51.800
of each of these scenarios at the moment,

00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:55.070
but we've been sort of trying to plan

00:36:55.070 --> 00:36:56.870
out all the various
options and recognizing

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:58.100
the complexity of the problem,

00:36:58.100 --> 00:36:59.477
and we want to present the president

00:36:59.477 --> 00:37:02.800
and the provost with an adequate map

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:04.440
for making this decision.

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:05.940
Now Peter and Scott and I,

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.370
and all of our faculty and staff

00:37:07.370 --> 00:37:09.710
are committed to Yale's
educational mission.

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:12.340
It's never been more
important than it is now.

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:16.410
Yale has been through many
crises in its history,

00:37:16.410 --> 00:37:18.120
and this is, as far as I'm concerned,

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:19.530
it's the biggest crisis I've ever seen,

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:21.120
and I imagine it's the biggest crisis

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:23.270
that many of us have ever encountered.

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:24.860
But we're a strong community and I think

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:26.860
we will come through this and ensure

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:29.710
that all Yale students
get an excellent education

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:31.210
in the years to come.

00:37:31.210 --> 00:37:32.827
So thank you, Peter and Scott.

00:37:32.827 --> 00:37:36.060
- Great, thank you very much, Pericles.

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:38.553
Kim, I wanna throw this back to you.

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:42.800
In your role as vice
president for university life,

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:46.470
what concerns have your
team been working on

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:48.100
and preparing for,

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:51.960
especially in this context
of so much uncertainty?

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:53.550
Kim.

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:55.750
- So what I was saying
was that one of the things

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:57.710
our staff hears a lot about are people

00:37:57.710 --> 00:38:00.980
feeling a loss of people and place,

00:38:00.980 --> 00:38:03.400
because we're all scattered
all over New Haven,

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:05.490
all over the state, many of us,

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:07.460
all over the country and the world.

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:10.170
And we miss, people talk
about missing interacting

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:11.980
with their colleagues
and their classmates,

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:13.360
pursuing their work and activities,

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:15.410
and actually physically being on campus

00:38:15.410 --> 00:38:16.790
and in New Haven.

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:19.170
And Zoom sometimes just
doesn't do it for everybody.

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:23.660
I know for me, I miss
seeing Trey, and Jennifer,

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:26.340
and Felix, and the custodial staff

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:28.050
and SSS when I come in.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:30.600
Or, like many of you, I'm
tired of my own cooking.

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:33.220
I wouldn't mind going to a
dining hall at the moment,

00:38:33.220 --> 00:38:34.100
going to Berkeley College,

00:38:34.100 --> 00:38:35.920
which I go to a lot and seeing Lynette

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:37.220
and having a good meal.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:38.530
Or even walking around the grounds

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:40.270
of the facility so (mumbles) prepares

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:41.710
around this time of year.

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:42.900
So I know most of you are missing

00:38:42.900 --> 00:38:44.440
being here physically.

00:38:44.440 --> 00:38:47.620
But we have to sustain
a sense of community,

00:38:47.620 --> 00:38:48.920
and we have a lot of staff who are working

00:38:48.920 --> 00:38:50.510
very hard to do that.

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:53.000
We recognize and hear about challenges,

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:54.880
and certainly we've all
talked about the fact

00:38:54.880 --> 00:38:57.970
that people have concerns about childcare,

00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:00.070
about the internet
connections that they have

00:39:00.070 --> 00:39:01.650
for themselves and their children,

00:39:01.650 --> 00:39:03.870
and of course, the need
to support faculty,

00:39:03.870 --> 00:39:07.600
sorry, family members who are sick

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:09.310
or who may have lost jobs.

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:11.730
So we continue to support them as well.

00:39:11.730 --> 00:39:13.780
And we're paying particular attention to,

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:18.360
in particular, mental
health and wellbeing.

00:39:18.360 --> 00:39:20.920
We have a lot of colleagues
around the university

00:39:20.920 --> 00:39:22.780
who are working very hard to support

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:24.630
the community on that.

00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:26.800
The Being Well at Yale program

00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:28.440
which supports staff and faculty

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:31.400
has been putting a lot
of its programs online,

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:33.420
and they report that there
are increased numbers

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:36.350
of people dropping in to
the meditation sessions,

00:39:36.350 --> 00:39:39.460
and also to stretching exercise classes,

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:40.920
which is good to hear.

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:44.850
We continue to support
students who might have needs

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:46.220
for mental health and counseling.

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:50.220
And that particular
program has been receiving

00:39:50.220 --> 00:39:54.660
700 calls with students every week,

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:56.240
so they're being really
helpful to make sure

00:39:56.240 --> 00:39:58.100
the students are dealing
with whatever anxiety

00:39:58.100 --> 00:40:01.740
or other issues they have
as they complete their term.

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:04.350
Now, we recognize that
there are other kinds

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:07.340
of challenges that are being exasperated

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:09.320
by this particular health crisis.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:11.130
Dean Brown mentioned one of them.

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:15.980
COVID-19 is affecting
different populations

00:40:15.980 --> 00:40:19.110
differently based on race
and socioeconomic status,

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.740
as she mentioned, including
here in New Haven.

00:40:21.740 --> 00:40:23.380
We're conscious of that as it plays

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:26.400
out in the community, the
campus community as well.

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:29.160
And of course, there is news nationally

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:30.960
of the rise in racial discrimination

00:40:32.915 --> 00:40:34.910
against Asians and Asian Americans,

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:38.650
and we want people to know
that we have resources

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:40.300
available to support people,

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:41.890
because we really want to support those

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:44.790
who are facing harassment
and discrimination.

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:47.810
So we have a team of people
around the university

00:40:47.810 --> 00:40:49.450
who are working very hard to make sure

00:40:49.450 --> 00:40:51.620
that the community is taken care of,

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:53.410
and that we can connect with each other.

00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:57.930
I encourage you, all of
us, to support each other

00:40:57.930 --> 00:41:00.390
as we go through this process of trying to

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:02.530
not only continue to flatten the curve,

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:04.190
but also to prepare to turn the corner

00:41:04.190 --> 00:41:06.817
that both you and Scott have described.

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:11.370
- Great, thank you very much, Kim.

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:15.220
Kim, Nancy, Scott, Pericles,
I want to thank you all

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:17.233
for what you're doing for our community.

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:20.610
Kim, I'd be interested in
hearing some of the questions

00:41:20.610 --> 00:41:22.570
that you've been collecting
from participants,

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:25.140
so why don't we turn
to those questions now.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:25.973
- Good.

00:41:25.973 --> 00:41:28.140
So a lot of people submitted questions,

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:29.340
so thank you, everybody.

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:32.750
The first question is for Nancy.

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:35.177
Nancy, the question you're being asked is,

00:41:35.177 --> 00:41:38.377
"When faculty, students,
and staff return to campus,

00:41:38.377 --> 00:41:40.337
"what will we do to reduce the possible

00:41:40.337 --> 00:41:43.887
"transmission of COVID-19
in terms of cleaning,

00:41:43.887 --> 00:41:46.867
"social distancing, (mumbles) and alike?"

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:52.423
Thank you for the question, yeah.

00:41:52.423 --> 00:41:53.760
- Okay.

00:41:53.760 --> 00:41:57.320
So first I would say we will adhere

00:41:57.320 --> 00:41:59.730
to current state and city guidelines

00:41:59.730 --> 00:42:01.320
at the time that we reopen,

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:04.550
and these in turn will
depend on the prevalence

00:42:04.550 --> 00:42:06.090
of infection at that time.

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:08.080
And so we may need to make changes

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:10.460
as the characteristics of the epidemic

00:42:10.460 --> 00:42:12.670
and that guidance changes.

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:15.960
I'd say social distancing
is the fundamental strategy,

00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:17.960
and it's going to include modifying

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:20.560
our physical spaces and
our movement pattern

00:42:20.560 --> 00:42:22.950
so that we increase
distances in the classrooms,

00:42:22.950 --> 00:42:25.880
in our labs, our offices,
the dining halls,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:27.873
our common spaces in the dorms.

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:32.190
Depending on the guidance
and prevalence of infection,

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:34.820
we may need to require masks,

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:38.000
and we may need to limit large gatherings.

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:40.230
Our colleagues in
Environmental Health services

00:42:40.230 --> 00:42:44.150
are already developing
plans for enhanced cleaning

00:42:44.150 --> 00:42:46.700
through the school and the dorms.

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:48.820
Yale Health, as you know, offers testing

00:42:48.820 --> 00:42:50.910
and contact tracing for individuals

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:52.363
who develop symptoms.

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:54.840
And I think it's going
to be very important

00:42:54.840 --> 00:42:57.017
that we facilitate the
ability of our students

00:42:57.017 --> 00:42:59.620
and our faculty and staff to self-isolate

00:42:59.620 --> 00:43:03.770
and also to obtain medical care as needed,

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:06.610
and of course, we've already
developed the systems

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:09.010
to do this for our health care workers

00:43:09.010 --> 00:43:10.460
over the last several months.

00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:13.303
- Thank you, Nancy.

00:43:14.460 --> 00:43:17.820
We have a lot of questions
related to research.

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:19.997
So Scott, I'll ask you,

00:43:19.997 --> 00:43:21.913
"When do you think
research labs will open,

00:43:21.913 --> 00:43:23.687
"and how can we open them safely?"

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:26.490
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:29.730
Like most of the people
that asks that question,

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:31.540
it's a question I'd like
to know the answer to

00:43:31.540 --> 00:43:35.840
because, I like others,
I'm a faculty member

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:38.783
who has a research lab
whose work was suspended.

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:41.730
We had just received a new federal grant

00:43:41.730 --> 00:43:44.600
to do work that we were
excited to perform,

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:48.160
but in order to follow
public health guidelines,

00:43:48.160 --> 00:43:49.860
that work has stopped,

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:53.230
and the lab has been been cleared.

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:55.310
But of course, we, like
others, are anxious

00:43:55.310 --> 00:43:57.530
to get that work started again,

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:00.483
but we need to do it in a safe way.

00:44:01.830 --> 00:44:04.500
And it's not just work in laboratories,

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:06.640
it's also work that's done in libraries

00:44:06.640 --> 00:44:09.830
with collections and access to the various

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:12.620
and amazing resources that are available

00:44:12.620 --> 00:44:16.433
to the faculty staff and
students at the university.

00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:20.170
It's important that we
move carefully, safely,

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:21.870
and within public health guidelines

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:23.290
that Nancy just described,

00:44:23.290 --> 00:44:25.590
and also in compliance
with government policy.

00:44:26.608 --> 00:44:29.450
I was interested to hear
that Governor Lamont

00:44:29.450 --> 00:44:31.280
emphasized that university research

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:33.170
is one of the functions
that he would like to see

00:44:33.170 --> 00:44:35.080
reopened soon in an announcement he made

00:44:35.080 --> 00:44:36.963
in the past day.

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:41.640
We expect we'll be able to
reactivate research labs

00:44:41.640 --> 00:44:44.160
and the libraries before we have students

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:45.710
in the classrooms and the dining halls,

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:47.490
as I mentioned before.

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:49.070
The densities are lower,

00:44:49.070 --> 00:44:52.110
it's easier to socially distance.

00:44:52.110 --> 00:44:54.160
It's often the case
that personal protective

00:44:54.160 --> 00:44:55.990
equipment is worn in these spaces,

00:44:55.990 --> 00:44:57.970
particularly in the laboratories,

00:44:57.970 --> 00:45:00.850
and so we anticipate
that we'll be able to,

00:45:00.850 --> 00:45:04.190
something that we can do
before the fall semester

00:45:04.190 --> 00:45:06.440
and hopefully substantially
sooner than that.

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:09.550
As I described, there
are Research Continuity

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:12.270
Committees being chaired by Mike Crair

00:45:12.270 --> 00:45:13.840
and Susan Gibbons,

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:15.680
and their deliberations have gone on

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:17.600
for about almost two weeks,

00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:21.460
and we expect the report will be completed

00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:23.890
and released in the next week or two.

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:27.193
They have worked very
hard and very effectively.

00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:30.530
Let's be clear, this
will not be an immediate

00:45:30.530 --> 00:45:33.000
return to work, and the
work that we're doing

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:35.160
will not look like it
did when we were working

00:45:35.160 --> 00:45:36.843
in those spaces in February.

00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:40.200
After that report is released,

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:41.640
that guidance is released,

00:45:41.640 --> 00:45:43.820
each research group will need to consider

00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:47.580
how their operation can be achieved

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:50.950
within the safe and
operational requirements

00:45:50.950 --> 00:45:53.620
of the phased reactivation
that will be described

00:45:53.620 --> 00:45:55.720
in this committee report.

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:59.370
Each group will need to think
through those questions.

00:45:59.370 --> 00:46:01.740
But let's also be clear,

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:05.670
we will reactivate our
labs and libraries soon.

00:46:05.670 --> 00:46:08.230
We will do it safely and we will follow

00:46:08.230 --> 00:46:12.450
public health guidance and
government recommendations.

00:46:12.450 --> 00:46:15.213
Yale will return to
(mumbles) productivity.

00:46:17.120 --> 00:46:18.400
- Thank you, Scott.

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:22.443
The next set of questions
have to do with financial aid.

00:46:23.337 --> 00:46:26.277
"Will financial aid be
affected in the fall,

00:46:26.277 --> 00:46:28.067
"and will Yale college financial aid

00:46:28.067 --> 00:46:31.780
"be re-evaluated given the
economic circumstances?"

00:46:31.780 --> 00:46:33.108
Peter, I think that's for you.

00:46:33.108 --> 00:46:33.941
- Okay.

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:37.980
Thank you for that question
about financial aid.

00:46:37.980 --> 00:46:40.280
Look, the university has taken

00:46:40.280 --> 00:46:42.560
a substantial financial hit.

00:46:42.560 --> 00:46:46.570
However, and I wanna be
crystal clear about this,

00:46:46.570 --> 00:46:50.050
our commitment to our
need-based financial aid

00:46:50.050 --> 00:46:52.373
policies is unwavering.

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:57.090
Now students in Yale
college, if you're watching,

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:00.000
if your family circumstances have changed,

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.440
you should bring that
information to the attention

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:05.570
of the Office of
Undergraduate Financial Aid.

00:47:05.570 --> 00:47:07.020
They'll be ready to help you.

00:47:08.803 --> 00:47:10.103
- Great, thank you, Peter.

00:47:11.170 --> 00:47:13.010
I see we have a lot of questions,

00:47:13.010 --> 00:47:14.660
very specific questions from students

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:18.570
about leave policies, looks like housing,

00:47:18.570 --> 00:47:21.470
retrieving and storing
belongings left on campus,

00:47:21.470 --> 00:47:23.173
grading in the fall and the like.

00:47:24.220 --> 00:47:26.390
Students, I would say that your deans

00:47:26.390 --> 00:47:28.790
are all looking at many of these issues,

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:31.150
and they will be communicating
with you directly

00:47:31.150 --> 00:47:32.380
in the weeks and months ahead.

00:47:32.380 --> 00:47:34.093
So look for those emails.

00:47:36.600 --> 00:47:40.000
We also have a lot of
questions about commencement,

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:42.900
and I wanna let you know
that we are definitely

00:47:42.900 --> 00:47:44.830
going to be welcoming the Class of 2020

00:47:44.830 --> 00:47:47.470
back in person to campus.

00:47:47.470 --> 00:47:49.190
We just don't know exactly
when because we have to wait

00:47:49.190 --> 00:47:51.010
for public health guidelines to tell us

00:47:51.010 --> 00:47:54.330
when we can invite several
thousand people back

00:47:54.330 --> 00:47:57.360
to be here for traditional
Yale ceremonies.

00:47:57.360 --> 00:48:00.230
But when we know that,
we will let you know.

00:48:00.230 --> 00:48:01.410
And I do wanna tell you
that we're gonna have

00:48:01.410 --> 00:48:02.700
all the pomp and circumstances

00:48:02.700 --> 00:48:06.310
that we normally would have
as a traditional ceremony.

00:48:06.310 --> 00:48:08.290
But we're also gonna celebrate you twice

00:48:08.290 --> 00:48:12.870
because we're gonna have
a special celebration

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:16.660
with President Salovey actually conferring

00:48:16.660 --> 00:48:19.270
your degrees May 18th,

00:48:19.270 --> 00:48:21.920
and we'll send you some more information

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:23.040
about that as well.

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:23.873
So look forward to that,

00:48:23.873 --> 00:48:25.730
and we look forward to welcoming you back

00:48:25.730 --> 00:48:28.193
in person later on.

00:48:30.240 --> 00:48:35.240
Okay, I think that might be
all the questions I can see.

00:48:35.290 --> 00:48:37.630
- Okay, thank you, Kim, appreciate that,

00:48:37.630 --> 00:48:39.820
and we appreciate those
who sent questions.

00:48:39.820 --> 00:48:42.470
We know there's others that are out there

00:48:42.470 --> 00:48:46.500
and we'll try to answer
them in other ways.

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:49.850
So let me conclude by saying this.

00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:52.833
At Yale, we're going to
continue to pursue our mission.

00:48:53.680 --> 00:48:57.800
We'll be educating students in the fall,

00:48:57.800 --> 00:49:00.440
we'll be conducting research more fully

00:49:00.440 --> 00:49:02.393
as soon as it is safe to do so.

00:49:03.780 --> 00:49:06.400
Our financial challenges are serious,

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:09.600
but with your help and
guided by our principles,

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:10.850
we can work through them.

00:49:12.730 --> 00:49:15.410
Someday we'll look back on this period,

00:49:15.410 --> 00:49:17.950
and we'll know that our university emerged

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:20.663
from it stronger and more unified,

00:49:22.010 --> 00:49:23.920
and that research universities like Yale

00:49:23.920 --> 00:49:27.560
made substantial
contributions to addressing

00:49:27.560 --> 00:49:30.013
this horrible global crisis.

00:49:31.290 --> 00:49:35.197
I wanna thank everyone for what you do

00:49:35.197 --> 00:49:38.690
and what you will continue
to do for our community,

00:49:38.690 --> 00:49:41.820
and for joining this conversation today.

00:49:41.820 --> 00:49:43.890
We'll keep you updated quite regularly.

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:46.040
And we'll do that through email,

00:49:46.040 --> 00:49:48.010
we'll do that through another town hall

00:49:48.010 --> 00:49:50.340
that we are going to schedule,

00:49:50.340 --> 00:49:52.500
most likely for July,

00:49:52.500 --> 00:49:54.620
and we'll speak more at that time

00:49:54.620 --> 00:49:56.713
about the fall semester.

00:49:56.713 --> 00:49:57.546
In the meantime,

00:49:57.546 --> 00:50:00.480
as Kim suggested at the beginning of this,

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:02.433
please visit covid19.yale.edu,

00:50:03.850 --> 00:50:08.350
covid19.yale.edu for
the latest information.

00:50:08.350 --> 00:50:11.090
And to keep up with late breaking news,

00:50:11.090 --> 00:50:15.150
please look at Yale Today
in your morning email.

00:50:15.150 --> 00:50:18.290
I wanna thank everyone for
attending this town hall.

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:21.100
Thank you for all that you're doing

00:50:21.100 --> 00:50:24.060
under these challenging circumstances.

00:50:24.060 --> 00:50:25.083
Thank you so much.

